// src/comterp_/tests/timeobj.comt -- hr:min:sec recognized as a TimeObj

// funcs: colonlist time class print size at

// A colon chain that reaches exactly three elements is checked against
// hr:min:sec bounds -- minute and second to a clock face (0..59), hour
// left unbounded so an elapsed duration (25:00:00) still constructs -- and
// becomes a TimeObj if it fits, a plain coloned list otherwise (colonlist.comt
// tests 5/5b cover the plain-list side, out-of-bounds on purpose so they
// don't collide with this recognition). time(timeobj :hour|:minute|:second)
// reads a field back off it, the same shape date()'s :day/:month/:year
// already use for DateObj. Construction and print()ing are covered here
// directly; time.comt covers time()'s other, unrelated keywords (:raw,
// :mono, :ms, :us, :ns) for the current wall clock/monotonic reading.
//
// Two things a TimeObj recognition site can't do, both because
// ComTerp::next_command_is() only ever sees the ONE postfix token right
// after the command that's about to return -- not an arbitrary distance
// ahead, and never across a variable binding to a later statement:
//
//   - A chain that keeps extending past three elements (1:2:3:4) can't be
//     told "don't recognize yet, more is coming" going forward, because
//     the next literal (not the next ':') always sits between this call
//     and the following one in postfix.  Recognition still fires at
//     three, harmlessly: colon_unwind_timeobj() (listfunc.c) turns the
//     TimeObj back into its three elements the moment it shows up as a
//     later ':''s own left operand, so flattening continues exactly as
//     if recognition had never fired -- see test 6.
//   - x=0:4:8 followed by buf@x on a LATER statement has no way to see
//     that @ is coming; x becomes a TimeObj (0,4,8 are in-bounds) and
//     buf@x reads nil rather than slicing, since @ only recognizes an
//     un-recognized, still-coloned list -- see test 7.  Only str@lo:hi:cap
//     written directly in the same expression is protected (colonslice.comt).

ok=true

// --- 1: hr:min:sec within bounds becomes a TimeObj, not a plain list --
//        assigned, not bare -- this is also the regression case for
//        ComTerp::next_command_is()'s post-eval cursor (comterp.c):
//        colon_triple_is_timeobj()'s own @ check runs inside the
//        assignment's post_eval operand span, where _pfoff alone would
//        answer about whatever follows the WHOLE assignment, not the
//        next token within the span ---
t1=1:8:30
ok=ok&&(class(t1)=="TimeObj")
ok=ok&&(print(t1 :str)=="1:8:30")
print("1 t1=1:8:30 (expect TimeObj 1:8:30): %v %v\n" class(t1) t1)

// --- 2: time(t :hour), time(t :minute), time(t :second) read the three
//        fields back off a TimeObj, the same shape date()'s :day/:month/
//        :year already use for DateObj ---
ok=ok&&(time(t1 :hour)==1 && time(t1 :minute)==8 && time(t1 :second)==30)
print("2 time(t1 :hour/:minute/:second) (expect 1 8 30): %v %v %v\n" time(t1 :hour) time(t1 :minute) time(t1 :second))

// --- 3: hour is left unbounded -- an elapsed duration past a clock's
//        24 hours still constructs, only minute/second are clamped to
//        a clock face ---
t3=25:30:0
ok=ok&&(class(t3)=="TimeObj" && time(t3 :hour)==25)
print("3 t3=25:30:0 elapsed hour>24 (expect TimeObj 25): %v %v\n" class(t3) time(t3 :hour))

// --- 4: minute or second outside 0..59 doesn't recognize -- falls
//        through to the ordinary flattened colon list per the guiding
//        rule (colonlist.comt's own header): a colon operator that
//        doesn't recognize its literal just returns a list ---
t4=1:70:30
ok=ok&&(class(t4)==nil && size(t4)==3 && t4@0==1 && t4@1==70 && t4@2==30)
print("4 t4=1:70:30 (bad minute, expect plain list 1:70:30): %v %v\n" class(t4) t4)

// --- 5: landing on @ still builds a cap-slice, never a TimeObj, even
//        when the three numbers happen to look like a valid clock time
//        -- next_command_is(`at) excludes this before recognition ever
//        checks bounds (colon_triple_is_timeobj, listfunc.c) ---
buf5="a123456789 more padding to be long enough"
s5=buf5@1:8:30
ok=ok&&(s5=="1234567" && size(s5)==7)
print("5 buf5@1:8:30, in-bounds-looking cap-slice stays a slice (expect 1234567): %v\n" s5)

// --- 6: a chain that keeps extending past three elements unwinds a
//        premature TimeObj back into a list and keeps flattening --
//        1,8,30 alone would be a TimeObj (test 1), but a fourth element
//        means it wasn't finished after all ---
t6=1:8:30:99
ok=ok&&(class(t6)==nil && size(t6)==4)
ok=ok&&(t6@0==1 && t6@1==8 && t6@2==30 && t6@3==99)
print("6 t6=1:8:30:99, unwound back to a list (expect nil 1:8:30:99): %v %v\n" class(t6) t6)

// --- 7: assign-then-use can't be helped -- x becomes a TimeObj (its
//        own three elements are in-bounds), so buf@x no longer sees a
//        plain coloned list to slice with, and reads nil.  Accepted,
//        documented limitation (doc/SLICES.md), not a bug: only a
//        str@lo:hi(:cap) written directly in the same expression is
//        protected, the same way the mid-chain unwind above only
//        protects a chain built in one expression ---
buf7="a123456789 more padding to be long enough"
x7=1:8:30
ok=ok&&(class(x7)=="TimeObj")
y7=buf7@x7
ok=ok&&(y7==nil)
print("7 x7=1:8:30 then buf7@x7 (expect TimeObj nil): %v %v\n" class(x7) y7)

print("timeobj: %v\n" ok)
ok
